15T00:00:00Z","closed_at":"2025-09-18T17:13:14Z"},"comments":0,"created_at":"2025-03-31T06:37:40Z","updated_at":"2025-04-09T23:20:34Z","closed_at":"2025-04-09T23:18:13Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducible in Godot 4.3*\n- Reproducible in Godot 4.4.1*\n- Reproducible in Godot 4.5.dev (latest master)\n\n*Reported by other users in the community.\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.5.dev (e585e6a3e) - Windows 11 (build 26100) - Multi-window, 1 monitor - OpenGL 3 (Compatibility) - NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050 Laptop GPU (NVIDIA; 31.0.15.5339) - 11th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-11400H @ 2.70GHz (12 threads)\n\n### Issue description\n\nTabbing into a different scene while editing a SpriteFrames resource's FPS causes the editor to crash.\n\nThe root cause is a null pointer error on [this line](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/e585e6a3eb35a73aae34786fc18b0824d4050428/editor/plugins/sprite_frames_editor_plugin.cpp#L1304) in SpriteFramesEditor. When changing scenes, the SpriteFrames local to the scene is disposed, causing `Ref<SpriteFrames>` to become null. This happens after SpriteFramesEditor closes, but before it is destroyed.\n\nNote that a similar bug also happens when you try to edit Frame Durations in the same way.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Create two scenes.\n2. In the first scene, create an AnimatedSprite2D node, and add a new SpriteFrames resource to it. (Don't save as file)\n3. Edit the SpriteFrames resource's FPS, and keep the SpinBox focused. (Don't click anywhere else yet)\n4. Tab to the second scene by clicking.\n5. Editor crashes.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nN/A","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_